 ## Background
-Uniform Ray Marching is a fundamental technique in volume rendering, often used as a baseline or fallback implementation [G]. It appears in various contexts: as a debug mode for direct atlas sampling in webGPU-native renderers [S1, unofficial], as a JAX-based volumetric renderer component [S2, unofficial], as a PyTorch fallback in Neural Sparse Voxel Fields (NSVF) [S3, unofficial], and in coronal NeRF rendering for solar physics [S4, unofficial]. The method relies on basic ray-AABB intersection and linear interpolation, forming the basis for more complex adaptive strategies [G].
+Uniform Ray Marching appears in various contexts: as a debug mode for direct atlas sampling in webGPU-native renderers [S1, unofficial], as a JAX-based volumetric renderer component [S2, unofficial], as a PyTorch fallback in Neural Sparse Voxel Fields (NSVF) [S3, unofficial], and in coronal NeRF rendering for solar physics [S4, unofficial].
 
